  "title": "Week 50: Maarjamäe Palace, Sagadi Forest, and Palmse Manor",
  "date": "2025-12-06T22:00:00.000Z",
  "content": "Week 50: Maarjamäe Palace, Sagadi Forest, and Palmse Manor\n\n🇪🇪 Estonian History Museum (Maarjamäe Palace)\n\nTo wrap up my Erasmus project, I visited the Estonian History Museum, spectacularly housed in the Maarjamäe Palace. This location offers a breathtaking vantage point for capturing a full panorama of Tallinn city! 📸✨\n\ncity view\n\n\n\nThe history museum's exhibitions are structured chronologically, starting right from the Estonian War of Independence.\n\ncastle\n\nTimeline Highlights: I saw exhibits showcasing daily life, from traditional city homes to farm life, and the evolution of clothing from the Independence War era to local traditional wear.\n\n| Period/Theme | Description |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| Independence Era | Uniforms and daily life during the War of Independence. |\n| Farm Life | Artifacts depicting rural Estonian life. |\n| Traditional Clothing | Examples of local attire. |\n\ncity home\n\nfarm life\n\nclothes in independence war\n\nclothes in independence war\n\nlocals clothes\n\nThe Soviet time section featured some fascinating (and mysterious) artifacts, like these vases whose purpose I couldn't quite decipher. 🤔\n\nSoviet time exhibits\n\nlocal town\n\nThe modern section included powerful displays on local protests and political resistance. ✊\n\nlocal contest record\n\nThe courtyard outside is a poignant collection of Soviet-era sculptures. It was unsettling to learn that some of these were tragically stolen or melted down by locals for scrap money. 😔\n\nback yard\n\nstolen head\n\n📽️ Estonian Film Museum\n\nLocated near the History Museum, the Film Museum underscores cinema's significant role in Estonian culture. It's filled with remarkable film equipment and interactive displays. 🎬\n\nA look at various film devices and the magic of filmmaking.\n\nGetting a glimpse behind the scenes with the green screen setup for Computer-Generated Imagery (CGI).\n\nFilm devices\n\ngreen screen\n\n🌳 Sagadi Forest Museum\n\nAs part of the Erasmus project, a visit to the Sagadi Forest Museum was essential, given that forests cover over 50% of Estonia! This museum beautifully illustrates the natural landscape and the traditional ways locals utilized the forest resources.\n\nThe entrance features a charming restaurant, very nice black garlic ice cream. 🍽️\n\nExhibits include specialized clothing for various forest-related trades:\n\n| Profession | Attire Displayed |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| Lumberjack | Rugged, practical work clothes 🪓 |\n| Beekeeper | Protective gear for honey collection 🐝 |\n\nblack garlic ice cream\n\nmuseum restauront\n\nlumberjack\n\nbeekeeper\n\nA captivating artwork combined a large-scale Extended Reality (XR) environment with Estonian folk sounds. It created a beautifully minimalistic and calming atmosphere—it genuinely reminded me of a MUJI store! (By the way, there's an Estonian MUJI playlist.)\n\nartwork in museum\n\n🖼️ Palmse Manor\n\nPalmse Manor stands as one of the most magnificent baroque mansions in all of Estonia, a truly grand piece of architectural history! 👑\n\nThe exterior is stunning, set against the Estonian landscape.\n\nThe interiors, including the main hall, are richly decorated.\n\nWe had a special treat when one of our group members performed a perfect piano concert in the museum—a beautiful moment! 🎹🎶\n\nmenor\n\nmain hall\n\npiano\n\nInside the Manor:\n\nLiving Room: Elegant and historically furnished.\n\nReading Room: A quiet, scholarly retreat.\n\nUnique Details: A delicate bird cage and a impressive wine collection added to the manor's charm. 🍷🐦\n\nliving room\n\nreading room\n\nbird cage\n\nwine collection",
